ou}, title = {{O}ptimization methods for frame conditioning and application to graph {L}aplacian scaling}, year = {2016}, eprint = {1609.02233}, note = {arXiv:1609.02233v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nA frame is scalable if each of its vectors can be rescaled in such a way that the resulting set becomes a Parseval frame. In this paper, we consider four different optimization problems for determining if a frame is scalable. We offer some algorithms to solve these problems. We then apply and extend our methods to the problem of reweighing (finite) graph so as to minimize the condition number of the resulting Laplacian.\n\n  * Harmonic functions and the spectrum of the Laplacian on the Sierpinski carpet\n\nMatthew Begue, Tristan Kalloniatis, Robert S. Strichartz\n\nJan 26 2012 math-ph math.AP math.MP arXiv:1201.5136v1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Balan_R in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ABalan_R+in%3Amath)\nciteturn0search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; ~Okoudjou}, title = {{O}ptimization methods for frame conditioning and application to graph {L}aplacian scaling}, year = {2016}, eprint = {1609.02233}, note = {arXiv:1609.02233v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\nIn classification they are mainly used as feature extractors. Ideally, we expect similar features when the inputs are from the same class. That is, we hope to see a small change in the feature vector with respect to a deformation on the input signal. This can be established mathematically, and the key step is to derive the Lipschitz properties. Further, we establish that the stability results can be extended for more general networks. We give a formula for computing the Lipschitz bound, and compare it with other methods to show it is closer to the optimal value.\n\n  * Optimization methods for frame conditioning and application to graph Laplacian scaling\n\nRadu Balan, Mathew Begué, Chae Clark, Kasso A. Okoudjou\n\nSep 